    {
      "id": "library/10665-90448/blocks/prose-sec-002",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Notification: name-based surveillance",
      "pageStart": 9,
      "pageEnd": 9,
      "target": "../sections/sec-002-a-notification-name-based-surveillance.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Notification: name-based surveillance\nGuidance 1. Name-based HIV case reporting (as contrasted with anonymous or coded reporting) can be \njustified only if data can be maintained in a confidential manner. The data should be used only for purposes \nrelated to public health (and not for discrimination or criminalization). Public health registries must be \ngoverned by the strictest rules of data protection and confidentiality.\nGuidance 2. Data should be shared with other public health agencies only if adequate security measures \nare in place and the reason is justified by a legitimate public heal",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Gives guidance that name-based HIV case reporting is justified only with strict confidentiality and use solely for public health, and that data be shared only with adequate security and the stricter privacy standard.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-90448/blocks/prose-sec-003",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Informed consent",
      "pageStart": 9,
      "pageEnd": 10,
      "target": "../sections/sec-003-b-informed-consent.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Informed consent\nGuidance 3. All surveillance classified as research should be reviewed by ethical review boards (or human \nsubjects’ research oversight committees) to ensure that the consent process fully informs potential \nparticipants about:\n\n\nthe public health goals of the study, \n\n\ntheir freedom to decline to participate, \n\n\nthe need to ask sensitive questions about sex and other behaviours linked to HIV transmission, \n\n\nthe nature of the risks and benefits of participation, \n\n\nthe availability of services to assist the individual with any negative consequence of participation,",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Sets out guidance on informed consent for HIV surveillance, requiring ethics review of research surveillance and listing what participants must be told, including risks, benefits and freedom to decline. It also addresses community consultation and opt-out approaches, citing the Kenya AIDS Indicator Survey.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-90448/blocks/prose-sec-004",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Vulnerable populations",
      "pageStart": 10,
      "pageEnd": 11,
      "target": "../sections/sec-004-c-vulnerable-populations.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Vulnerable populations\nGuidance 7. Understanding the patterns of sexual and drug-using behaviour among adolescents justifies \nstudies involving adolescents. However, they must understand the risks and benefits of participating in \nsurveillance activities. Explaining the risks and benefits is often possible among those aged 15–18 years.3 \nChildren below the age of 15 years, who lack the capacity to understand the risks and benefits of participation, \ncan only participate with parental consent. Such studies should provide, through referral, \n\n\npsychological and social support services, if need",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Gives guidance on involving vulnerable populations in HIV surveillance, including adolescents, who may consent from around 15 while younger children need parental consent and support services. It also covers groups such as sex workers, people who use drugs and men who have sex with men, stressing protection from legal harm and limits of confidentiality.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-90448/blocks/prose-sec-005",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Unlinked anonymous testing (UAT)",
      "pageStart": 11,
      "pageEnd": 11,
      "target": "../sections/sec-005-d-unlinked-anonymous-testing-uat.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Unlinked anonymous testing (UAT)\nGuidance 13. Unlinked anonymous testing (UAT) occurs when persons are tested for HIV but the test and \nresults are not linked to any identifying information. The participants are not provided with their results. \nUAT is often done without informed consent. It should be used for surveillance only when data from clinical \nsettings and other studies cannot provide the information necessary for surveillance. All study proposals \nusing UAT should be reviewed by committees capable of evaluating both the epidemiological and ethical \nissues involved. Those who conduct",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Gives guidance on unlinked anonymous HIV testing, which should be used only when other data cannot meet surveillance needs, be reviewed for ethical and epidemiological soundness and offer participants a way to learn their status.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-90448/blocks/prose-sec-006",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Data use",
      "pageStart": 11,
      "pageEnd": 12,
      "target": "../sections/sec-006-e-data-use.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Data use\nGuidance 17. Surveillance efforts can be justified only if they will help prevent the spread of HIV or will be \nused for directing resources to those already infected. When there is resistance to effective interventions or \nresources are scarce, surveillance may be used as a basis for advocacy and empowering the most vulnerable \npopulations. \nGuidance 18. The results of surveillance must always be communicated to stakeholders and the community. \nAny censoring of surveillance data must be challenged. Community advisory boards, ethics review \ncommittees and other oversight bodies charge",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Gives guidance that HIV surveillance is justified only if it helps prevention or directs resources, that results must be shared with communities, and that people at risk should be referred to services or the project should advocate for them.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-90448/blocks/prose-sec-007",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Introduction",
      "pageStart": 12,
      "pageEnd": 14,
      "target": "../sections/sec-007-1-introduction.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Why do we need ethical guidelines for second generation surveillance?\nIn 2000, the World Health Organization (WHO) and Joint United Nations Programme on HIV/AIDS (UNAIDS) \npublished the Guidelines for second generation HIV surveillance.(1) The guidelines are a series of modules \nreleased over the past decade, designed to address the complexity of different HIV epidemic contexts \nand meet the planning needs of national programmes. The earlier surveillance systems were focused on \nstrategies for determining the prevalence of both asymptomatic and symptomatic HIV infection. While \nthey helped to",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Introduces the need for ethical guidelines for second generation HIV surveillance, which since 2000 has added behavioural data and focused on populations at higher risk. It argues that accurate surveillance is essential to control the epidemic and that there may be a duty to conduct it. It identifies the intended users, including ministries, NGOs, community advisory boards and ethics committees.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-90448/blocks/prose-sec-008",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Overview of national and international ethical guidelines on surveillance",
      "pageStart": 14,
      "pageEnd": 15,
      "target": "../sections/sec-008-b-overview-of-national-and-international-ethical.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Overview of national and international ethical guidelines on surveillance \nThe modern era of medical ethics began with the promulgation of the Nuremberg Code in 1947. Nuremberg \nmarked the first of three major phases in the evolution of medical ethics. The first twenty years or so \nafter Nuremberg marked the articulation of ethical principles governing research, with less effort given to \ndeveloping the means to enforce those principles. During the second phase of evolution, in the wake of \nscandals such as the Tuskegee syphilis study in the United States, a new emphasis was placed on creating",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Reviews national and international ethical guidelines relevant to surveillance, tracing medical ethics from the Nuremberg Code through phases of articulation and oversight following scandals like the Tuskegee study. It notes how guidelines treat epidemiological studies and the use of existing records and biological specimens.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-90448/blocks/prose-sec-009",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Medical ethics and the ethics of epidemiological research: a literature review",
      "pageStart": 15,
      "pageEnd": 17,
      "target": "../sections/sec-009-c-medical-ethics-and-the-ethics-of-epidemiologic.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Medical ethics and the ethics of epidemiological research: a literature review \nThe ethical principles for the protection of human subjects articulated in Nuremberg and Helsinki, as we have \nnoted, were based on a common set of principles, stressing the absolute need to prioritize the rights of the \nindividual over that of society.(16) Thus, there could be no exceptions to voluntary participation and informed \nconsent.(16) Nuremberg and Helsinki and the first efforts to adopt national guidelines on research ethics \nwere, after all, rooted in a distinct historical period of revelations of great",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Reviews literature on medical and epidemiological research ethics, noting that Nuremberg and Helsinki prioritized the individual over society with no exceptions to consent. It describes how later guidance, including CIOMS, adapted these principles to population research and stressed building ethical review capacity in host countries.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-90448/blocks/prose-sec-010",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Research, practice and surveillance",
      "pageStart": 17,
      "pageEnd": 18,
      "target": "../sections/sec-010-d-research-practice-and-surveillance.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Research, practice and surveillance\nWith so much attention devoted to the standards that ought to govern medical research and to the \nmechanisms that could best ensure ethical oversight, it was deemed crucial to distinguish that domain of \nclinical practice which was not subject to the same regulatory norms. \nThe Declaration of Helsinki (1964) drew “a fundamental distinction…between medical research for which \nthe aim is essentially diagnostic or therapeutic for a patient, and medical research, the essential object \nof which is purely scientific and without implying direct diagnostic or therap",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Discusses the distinction between research and practice, from the Declaration of Helsinki onward, and the difficulty of classifying surveillance. It concludes that the ethical guidelines should apply to second generation surveillance either way, with mechanisms needed if it is treated as practice.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-90448/blocks/prose-sec-011",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Public health and mandatory notification",
      "pageStart": 18,
      "pageEnd": 20,
      "target": "../sections/sec-011-e-public-health-and-mandatory-notification.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Public health and mandatory notification\nIn many nations, there is a long history of governmentally mandated and conducted public health surveillance \nthat has been the primary source of data relevant to the understanding of patterns of morbidity and mortality, \nor the evaluation of efforts designed to limit the impact of disease outbreaks and epidemics.  Where such \nsurveillance exists as a matter of law or official regulation, it typically imposes on specified individuals \n(generally health-care workers) or institutions the duty to report information about identifiable persons \nto public hea",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Examines mandatory public health notification, under which health workers must report identifiable cases to authorities by law, often without consent. It discusses how ethics bodies have accepted such breaches of consent for public health while recognizing deep disagreement in the case of HIV.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-90448/blocks/prose-sec-012",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Data collection for HIV surveillance",
      "pageStart": 20,
      "pageEnd": 22,
      "target": "../sections/sec-012-2-data-collection-for-hiv-surveillance.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Public health surveillance: the role of mandatory notification\nSurveillance was the foundation on which the new public health was built, not only in the United States \nbut also in Europe. The move toward the reporting of disease by name to health officials in the United \nStates was paralleled in Europe, driven in part by the cholera epidemics of the nineteenth century. As \ndisease notification began to emerge as a tool for epidemic control, it was bolstered by the more well-\nestablished reporting of vital statistics, the registration of births, marriages and deaths. While vital statistics \nwer",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Traces the history of name-based disease notification in the United States and Europe, rooted in nineteenth-century epidemic control and vital registration. It describes the shift from AIDS to HIV case reporting and the debate over names versus coded identifiers, including in resource-limited settings. It ends by discussing privacy when linking HIV data with other databases and restating guidance on name-based reporting and data sharing.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-90448/blocks/prose-sec-013",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Integrated biological and behavioural surveillance",
      "pageStart": 22,
      "pageEnd": 22,
      "target": "../sections/sec-013-b-integrated-biological-and-behavioural-surveill.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Integrated biological and behavioural surveillance",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "A section heading for integrated biological and behavioural surveillance, with no further text.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
