    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-90448/blocks/prose-sec-014",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Individuals, local context and the consent process",
      "pageStart": 22,
      "pageEnd": 24,
      "target": "../sections/sec-014-individuals-local-context-and-the-consent-proces.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "A central feature of second generation surveillance is the incorporation of behavioural studies designed \nto provide a better understanding of dynamics of the epidemic in low-level, concentrated and generalized \nstates.(66) In each instance, the goal is to understand who is at risk for infection; what social, cultural and \nstructural factors explain the vulnerability and the prospect of further transmission; what interventions have \nbeen effective or ineffective in stemming the spread of HIV.\nEvaluating a National Surveillance System\nBehavioural surveillance studies typically focus on groups a",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Discusses consent in behavioural studies within second generation HIV surveillance, which gather highly sensitive data on sexual practices and drug use from groups such as sex workers, men who have sex with men and people who inject drugs. It argues that subject selection must be epidemiologically justified so vulnerable groups are not unduly burdened. Participants must give fully informed, affirmative opt-in consent that takes account of local language, beliefs and history. Opt-out procedures are judged unacceptable for face-to-face studies.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-90448/blocks/prose-sec-015",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Role of community consultation in the consent process",
      "pageStart": 24,
      "pageEnd": 25,
      "target": "../sections/sec-015-role-of-community-consultation-in-the-consent-pr.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "As noted, researchers are obliged to protect the rights of individual subjects through an informed consent \nprocess. Both for surveillance involving contact with individuals as well as the investigation of already \nexisting records, as discussed above, an additional step will prove vital: consultation with those who may \nrepresent the community at large. In its report The ethics of research related to health care in developing \ncountries, the Nuffield Council on Bioethics noted that in some nations such community consultation is \nrequired as a matter of law. In addressing this issue, the US NB",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Argues that, alongside individual consent, consultation with community representatives is vital for HIV surveillance, citing the Nuffield Council and the US National Bioethics Advisory Commission. It notes that community permission cannot replace individual consent and gives the Kenya AIDS Indicator Survey as an example where late consultation lowered participation.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-90448/blocks/prose-sec-016",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Unique issues of consent with minors and the role of parents in the consent process",
      "pageStart": 25,
      "pageEnd": 25,
      "target": "../sections/sec-016-unique-issues-of-consent-with-minors-and-the-rol.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The very basic principles of research ethics, which seek to protect the right of individuals to not participate \nin investigations if that is their choice, also require the protection of vulnerable individuals who may not \nhave the psychological or legal capacity to choose. The definition of when an individual reaches the legal \nage of majority may differ among nations, but where that threshold has not been passed, parents are \nroutinely required to give consent for the participation of their children. Such consent does not automatically \nguarantee that children will participate. Depending on",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Examines consent for minors in HIV surveillance, where parental consent and child assent may conflict with the need to interview adolescents about sexual behaviour, citing CIOMS guidance. It sets out conditions under which adolescents might participate without parental permission and stresses access to counselling and support services.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-90448/blocks/prose-sec-017",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Impact of social context on the capacity for consent: prisons and clinical settings",
      "pageStart": 25,
      "pageEnd": 26,
      "target": "../sections/sec-017-impact-of-social-context-on-the-capacity-for-con.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Settings in which individuals receive medical care, such as prenatal clinics, STI clinics, TB treatment centres \nand hospitals, provide a natural context for second generation surveillance. Inevitably, patients who are \nrecruited to studies may believe that they have no alternative but to agree to participate. They may feel that \ntheir treatment will be compromised or interrupted if they choose to not participate in second generation \nsurveillance studies or if they choose to withdraw from studies after agreeing to join. As one research team \nnoted, “in developing countries, where many partici",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that patients in clinics and prisoners may feel unable to refuse surveillance studies, fearing their care or treatment will suffer. It recommends community consultation, having staff not involved in care obtain consent, and special scrutiny by ethics committees of prisoners' voluntary choice.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-90448/blocks/prose-sec-018",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Unique issues of consent with vulnerable populations",
      "pageStart": 26,
      "pageEnd": 27,
      "target": "../sections/sec-018-unique-issues-of-consent-with-vulnerable-populat.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The problem of the coercive impact of undue inducements extends beyond prison populations. In poor \nor marginalized populations, small offers or payments to potential participants in second generation \nsurveillance may be hard to refuse. In relatively wealthy countries, this is an issue that affects the \nimpoverished and marginalized minorities. In nations where poverty is widespread, offers by external \ninvestigators with access to resources may produce, however inadvertently, pressures to participate in \nsurveillance, which undermine the principle of voluntary consent. The 2002 CIOMS Interna",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Discusses how payments or services may unduly induce poor or marginalised people to join surveillance, citing CIOMS guidelines and noting the limits of local ethics review. It stresses informed, voluntary consent, enforceable confidentiality and clearly stated limits to confidentiality for the most-at-risk populations.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-90448/blocks/prose-sec-019",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Unique issues of consent with women",
      "pageStart": 27,
      "pageEnd": 28,
      "target": "../sections/sec-019-unique-issues-of-consent-with-women.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "The 2002 CIOMS International ethical guidelines for biomedical research involving human subjects \nmaintained that husbands or heads of households may not consent on behalf of women. Nonetheless, in \nmany societies, the father, husband or family head is expected to make all decisions regarding sensitive \nfamily issues. Women and other family members who fail to submit to male authority may be subject to \ndomestic violence, divorce or social ostracism. The refusal to involve women in studies because of such \ncultural constraints could thwart investigations crucial to women’s interests. In the Un",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Considers consent for women in societies where men are expected to decide, noting CIOMS's position that husbands may not consent on women's behalf and the NBAC's narrow exception allowing supplemental male permission. It concludes that a woman's own consent is required and that lack of male approval must never exclude her.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-90448/blocks/prose-sec-020",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Confidentiality",
      "pageStart": 28,
      "pageEnd": 29,
      "target": "../sections/sec-020-confidentiality.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "As noted, CIOMS, in its 2002 revision of International ethical guidelines for biomedical research involving \nhuman subjects, established an obligation on the part of investigators to safeguard the confidentiality of \nsubjects’ research data. The Nuffield Council on Bioethics also emphasized protecting the security and \nconfidentiality of data.(83) So too do the 2009 CIOMS Guidelines on epidemiological research.\nThe protection of confidentiality in the context of research and other surveillance-related investigations \nhas two sources. On a pragmatic level, the failure to provide such assurances",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Sets out the ethical and practical reasons for protecting confidentiality in HIV surveillance, citing CIOMS and the Nuffield Council and a study in Côte d'Ivoire where fear of disclosure lowered testing. It addresses data security and legal reporting requirements, stating that participants must be told of any limits to confidentiality.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-90448/blocks/prose-sec-021",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Anonymous unlinked surveillance",
      "pageStart": 29,
      "pageEnd": 32,
      "target": "../sections/sec-021-c-anonymous-unlinked-surveillance.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Anonymous unlinked surveillance\nWith the identification of HIV in 1984, it became clear that an understanding of the dimensions of the AIDS \nepidemic would require more than a careful detailing of the incidence and prevalence of overt symptoms \nof the disease. To enable public health authorities in nations with well-developed public health and health-\ncare systems to target and evaluate preventive interventions and plan for the health-care services that \nwould be required in the future as those with infection progressed to symptomatic conditions, knowledge \nof the prevalence of HIV infection w",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Traces the history and ethics of unlinked anonymous HIV testing of blood collected for other purposes, which became the backbone of surveillance in generalised epidemics. It describes how US authorities and a 1985 Hastings Center group judged it ethical, and how WHO's 1989 guidance supported it. The section then covers growing debate as treatment became available and the case for informing people of their status grew. It concludes that women in antenatal clinic surveillance must also be referred to prevention of mother-to-child transmission services.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-90448/blocks/prose-sec-022",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Individual consent to blood drawn specifically for HIV surveillance",
      "pageStart": 32,
      "pageEnd": 33,
      "target": "../sections/sec-022-individual-consent-to-blood-drawn-specifically-f.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "While much of the discussion on UAT had focused on ANCs and the needs and claims of women, there \nwas also increasing uncertainty about the ethics of UAT in population-based household surveys. Despite \nthe importance of studies drawn from ANCs, there was growing concern that they might not, in fact, \nprovide the most accurate characterization of the prevalence of HIV infection in the population at large. \nThey certainly did not permit the linkage of behavioural data with serostatus. Household surveys drawn \nfrom sample populations, although very expensive and complex, were thus deemed necessar",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Discusses consent for blood drawn specifically for HIV testing in household surveys, where direct contact with participants raises the question of how they should learn their results. It concludes that participants must be offered the chance to learn their results through survey teams, mobile units or local clinics.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-90448/blocks/prose-sec-023",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Use of collected data",
      "pageStart": 33,
      "pageEnd": 33,
      "target": "../sections/sec-023-3-use-of-collected-data.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "",
      "truncated": false,
      "provenance": "ingested",
