    {
      "id": "library/10665-90448/blocks/prose-sec-024",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Obligation to use data",
      "pageStart": 33,
      "pageEnd": 33,
      "target": "../sections/sec-024-a-obligation-to-use-data.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Obligation to use data\nThere is now international consensus that research in a given population is justified only if the results of \nstudies will provide benefit to the populations from which participants are drawn. Surveillance activities \nhave always been justified in terms of the population-level benefits that would follow. In the 2000 revision \nof the Declaration of Helsinki, the WMA declared, “Medical research is only justified if there is a reasonable \nlikelihood that the populations in which the research is carried out stand to benefit from the results of the \nresearch.”(102) The 2002 C",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Argues that surveillance, like research, is justified only if it benefits the populations studied, citing the Declaration of Helsinki and CIOMS, and that ethics committees should consider how data will be used. It notes that surveillance can support advocacy for vulnerable groups.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-90448/blocks/prose-sec-025",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Obligation to disseminate data",
      "pageStart": 33,
      "pageEnd": 34,
      "target": "../sections/sec-025-b-obligation-to-disseminate-data.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Obligation to disseminate data\nDissemination of findings represents a benefit to which the participants in surveillance-related activities \nhave an ethical claim. In stressing the imperative for researchers from developed countries to make good-\nfaith efforts to ensure that participants in a clinical trial have access to the products shown to be effective in \ncompleted research, the US NBAC stressed the importance of communicating results to the communities \nin which investigations take place. As one group of international researchers note, “At a minimum, if \nresearchers collect data in a coun",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Sets out the ethical obligation to share surveillance findings with participants and communities, citing NBAC, international researchers and the Nuffield Council. It adds guidance that, when results could harm the population, risks should be minimised in consultation with community representatives.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-90448/blocks/prose-sec-026",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Obligation to intervene at the community level",
      "pageStart": 34,
      "pageEnd": 34,
      "target": "../sections/sec-026-c-obligation-to-intervene-at-the-community-level.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Obligation to intervene at the community level\nResearchers and others who conduct surveillance may not be able to ensure that vulnerable communities \nhave access to the services that surveillance reveals would be helpful in preventing the spread of HIV, \nsuch as condoms, supportive services for women or needle exchange programmes. One function of pre-\nsurveillance consultation with community advisory boards and ethics review committees would be to \ndelineate the shared understanding of what obligations, if any, those who conduct surveillance will have in \nthis regard at the conclusion of the s",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Notes that surveillance teams may not be able to ensure services such as condoms or needle exchange, and gives guidance that investigators should agree in advance with community boards and ethics committees what advocacy role they will play.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-90448/blocks/prose-sec-027",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Obligation to intervene at the individual level",
      "pageStart": 34,
      "pageEnd": 36,
      "target": "../sections/sec-027-d-obligation-to-intervene-at-the-individual-leve.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Obligation to intervene at the individual level\nThose conducting integrated behavioural and biological studies and surveillance activities may, during the \ncourse of the interview, become aware that a participant is placing himself or herself at risk because of a \nlack of information about how HIV is acquired and transmitted, limited access to preventive services or \npsychosocial stressors. Individuals may also reveal that they themselves are being placed at risk because \nof the coercive or threatening behaviour of their sexual partners. While it may be beyond the immediate \nrole of the invest",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Discusses obligations when interviews reveal that participants are at risk from lack of information, lack of services or coercive partners, recommending referral to appropriate services. Where referrals are impossible, it says the project itself should advocate for services with ethical oversight bodies.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-90448/blocks/prose-sec-028",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Summary and Conclusions: ethical implications of type of epidemic",
      "pageStart": 36,
      "pageEnd": 38,
      "target": "../sections/sec-028-4-summary-and-conclusions-ethical-implications-o.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "implications of type of epidemic\nThroughout this discussion, the consultations  and in the guidelines that have emerged from it, a number \nof themes have been salient. Despite the emphasis on the importance of protecting the rights of individuals \nin the context of surveillance and the limits such concerns may have for surveillance as an activity, it is \nworth re-emphasizing that there is an ethical obligation to undertake surveillance precisely because of the \npotential for prevention of disease, suffering and death. It is here that the linkage between surveillance \nas a data-gathering activi",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Concludes that there is an ethical obligation to conduct HIV surveillance because of its potential to prevent disease and death, but notes the challenge of providing ethical oversight for surveillance outside research. It stresses confidentiality and consent given stigma and discrimination linked to HIV. It then explains how ethical concerns differ between low-level, concentrated and generalised epidemics.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
